<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Zero Company Mod Studio, rebuilt on Avalonia.

    A NEW PROJECT BESIDE THE OLD ONE, NOT A CONVERSION OF IT. The ImGui Studio was the tool the
    author used and shipped (up to 1.6.1); it kept working and kept building for the whole of this
    port, and nothing switched over until the replacement was actually better. From 2.0.0 THIS is
    the Studio the download carries, under the old one's executable name (see AssemblyName below),
    and the ImGui project stays in the solution as ZeroCompanyModStudio.Legacy, the reference for
    behaviour and wording while the last screens are ported.

    WHAT DOES NOT MOVE: UEToolkit.Core, referenced whole. 58,000 lines of project model, build,
    verify and authoring that never knew what was drawing it. That is the entire reason this port
    is a re-skin of a working tool rather than a rewrite of one — the hard half is already on the
    right side of the line.

    WHAT DOES NOT COME WITH IT: UEToolkit.App. It carries ImGui, AppState and the toolkit's own
    interface, and referencing it would drag the thing being replaced into its replacement.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <!--
      THIS IS THE STUDIO NOW: its executable is ZeroCompanyModStudio.exe, the name every guide
      tells people to run, so unzipping a release over an old folder replaces the old Studio.
      The ImGui project that used to own that name builds as ZeroCompanyModStudio.Legacy, so the
      two never write the same file. Nothing in the code looks the assembly up by name (the
      settings, the profile search and the data folder all start from AppContext.BaseDirectory,
      which is the folder, not the file), so the rename moves nothing a person has saved.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>ZeroCompanyModStudio</AssemblyName>
    <Product>Zero Company Mod Studio</Product>
    <AssemblyTitle>Zero Company Mod Studio</AssemblyTitle>
    <Company>Clonetrooper163</Company>
    <Authors>Clonetrooper163</Authors>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Clonetrooper163. Licensed under the GNU GPL v3.0.</Copyright>
    <!--
      THE VERSION IS IN TWO PLACES AND publish.ps1 REFUSES IF THEY DISAGREE: Branding.Version in
      Program.cs (what About and the update check read) and these two elements (what Windows shows
      on the exe's Details tab). Change all three together.
    -->
    <Version>2.0.0</Version>
    <InformationalVersion>2.0.0</InformationalVersion>
    <!--
      Without this the SDK appends "+<commit hash>" to InformationalVersion, and the exe's Details
      tab then reads "Product version 2.0.0+8d28296...": a number nobody downloading the Studio can
      use, and different on every machine that builds it. (publish.ps1's check accepts both forms.)
    -->
    <IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>false</IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>
    <!--
      The executable's own icon, the one the ImGui Studio carried: derived from the game's icon
      and marked so the tool and the game are not the same picture in a taskbar (see
      tools/studioicon.py). Linked from that project rather than copied, like the guides below.
    -->
    <ApplicationIcon>..\ZeroCompanyModStudio\ZeroCompanyModStudio.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <!-- The linked renderer pins buffers to hand GL raw pointers, as any GL code must. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\UEToolkit.Core\UEToolkit.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The Studio's developer command line - the flags that open a project, click through a tab,
    dump a mesh or photograph a viewport without a mouse (about three thousand lines of driver in
    MainWindow, App and ModelView) - is testing scaffolding and has no business in a release. It
    sits behind "#if DEV", gated the same way UEToolkit.App's harness is, so the two projects
    answer to one switch.

    Presence of ../dev/ is what enables it, rather than a build configuration anyone has to
    remember to pass. Locally the folder exists, DEV is defined, and every flag works - agents
    drive this Studio headless with them. On a fresh clone of the public source the folder is
    absent, DEV is not defined, and the driver compiles away, so the checkout BUILDS rather than
    failing on something it was never given.

    A RELEASE CAN TURN IT OFF, which the folder test alone could not: on THIS machine the folder
    always exists, so a publish from here shipped the whole driver in ZeroCompanyModStudio.dll
    while publish.ps1's DevHarness=false property only reached the other project. publish.ps1
    passes it for both now, and searches the published dll for the flag names before it calls a
    stage publishable.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(DevHarness)' != 'false' And Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\..\dev')">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);DEV</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE GUIDES, LINKED FROM THE ImGui STUDIO RATHER THAN COPIED. Same rule as the renderer above:
    never a second copy of the source. Two copies of a handbook can disagree about the tool, which
    is worse than having none, and the whole point of item five on the author's list is that they
    describe the right Studio.

    They are carried locally so the menu works with no network; the published link is only the
    fallback, because a local copy is never newer than the build it shipped with.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\ZeroCompanyModStudio\Guides\*.html"
             Link="Guides\%(Filename)%(Extension)"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE PROFILE TEMPLATE, LINKED FOR THE SAME REASON. The first run (UEToolkit.Core's GameSetup)
    makes the user's own profile from it: this file with PaksDirectory and MappingsFile filled in,
    saved as settings/active-profile.json. Without it beside the exe there is nothing to fill in,
    and the Studio opens with "could not prepare the game profile". It is never written to.
  -->
  <!--
    THE BASE MODS, which the ImGui Studio shipped and this project had dropped (found 2026-11-03 by the
    source snapshot's comparison). BaseMods\CustomWeaponClasses.json is the project the build installs
    first when a mod puts a weapon on a custom Armoury shelf (UEToolkit.Core BaseMod.Ensure reads it from
    AppContext.BaseDirectory\BaseMods). Without it such a build stops with "this build does not carry the
    'CustomWeaponClasses' project", for everyone who does not already have that mod installed.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\BaseMods\**\*.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" LinkBase="BaseMods" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\ZeroCompanyModStudio\profile-template.json"
             Link="profile-template.json"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE RENDERER, LINKED RATHER THAN COPIED — the same line the Level Editor's project already
    takes, and for the same reason: never a second copy of the source. Two copies of a renderer
    can disagree about an asset, which is exactly why SplashBmp moved into Core.

    These files are pure Silk.NET and CUE4Parse; not one of them mentions ImGui, AppState or the
    toolkit's interface, which is the only reason they can move at all. The Rendering folder is
    exactly the shared drawing code: the toolkit's own viewport (a GLFW window and an ImGui frame,
    which hosting the renderer inside Avalonia replaces) lives in UEToolkit.App\Viewport instead.

    It also means the shader the author has spent weeks making match the game — the material
    families, the trim sheet, the emissive rules — is the SAME shader here. "Default Lit" is not
    a new approximation; it is that one.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\UEToolkit.App\Rendering\*.cs" LinkBase="Shared\Rendering" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="12.1.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="12.1.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="12.1.2" />
    <!--
      Silk.NET drives the GL, exactly as it does in the toolkit and the Level Editor. Avalonia
      owns the CONTEXT and hands out a GetProcAddress; Silk binds to it. That is the whole reason
      the existing renderer can be reused rather than rewritten against a second graphics API.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Silk.NET.OpenGL" Version="2.22.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
